A||||7||feffer||2.5L in other cars
Z||000000||feffer||07-02-99||10:56 PM||||I just bought an '87 Fiero with the 2.5L. The car has 173K on it. I am told the original engine was cooked and replaced with one from another '87 car (now has about 60K on it), which I'm pretty sure was not a Fiero. I've certainly read an awful lot about what other cars the engine was used in, but what I really want to know is where MINE came from. The reason I say it's not out of a Fiero is that the oil filter is within the oil pan. I would think that narrows it down, for someone who knows more about it than I do, anyway. I do know that there is a three letter code stamped on the block, but I'd rather not dismantle half the engine to be able to see it. So, I'm trying the easy way first. Anyone know what car used the 2.5 with the oil filter inside the pan?
Z||000001||batboy||07-02-99||11:33 PM||||Very late in the model year of 1987, GM redesigned the 2.5L (for all models, I think). I don't remember everything that got changed, but going to an internal oil filter was one of the changes. I believe all the 1988 and newer 2.5 had that internal oil filter too, even the Fieros. I'm not sure there is any easy way to tell what the engine is out of, and to be honest, it probably don't really matter.
Z||000002||batboy||07-02-99||11:44 PM||||Ok, I looked it up in my Haynes Fiero manual. On page 258 it says that some 1987 and all 1988 four-cylinder engines are equipped with a force balancer, which necessitates a different location for the oil filter. On these models, an element-type oil filter in the oil pan replaces the spin-on type of filter located on the side of the engine block.
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||07-02-99||11:51 PM||||My 88 has it.....very messy
Z||000004||theogre||07-03-99||12:17 AM||||the balancer motor was not "standard" in 87 Fiero, but is compatible. I think the balancer motor started in 88. It was used in numerous GM cars. Only with the motor serial # can you maybe find what it was built into.<p>the filter in the pan is a pain in the ass to say the least. VW used to use this decades ago. what a pile of crap. who ever resurected that idea was sniffing something very bad.
Z||000005||lowCG||07-03-99||01:12 AM||||I don't believe there were any internal changes between the engines,per car issued.<br>The differences were in the intake manifolding,and controls(ignition,air/fuel mixture)if there were any.<br>Like others have stated,no difference,all made at the same plant,and shipped to the different assembly locations for each model.<br>Must've been the new guy who designed the oil filter into the pan.
Z||000006||feffer||07-03-99||12:00 PM||||I have that Haynes manual, but hadn't come across pg. 258 yet I guess. I'll have to check it out, thanks. I hadn't taken the filter out yet, since I figured it's got to be a mess. I wanted to have the new one when I take that one out, but I didn't know what to look for. So, I can just look up the 88 2.5L. It will be the same filter, right?<br>     Right now I am looking for new fuel lines. All the parts stores keep telling me to go to the dealer. Is the dealer going to laugh at me? I've heard they do still stock SOME parts. Could I try ANY GM parts dealer or should I stick to Pontiac? There's a couple Chevy and Olds dealers much nearer to me than a Pontiac one.
Z||000007||Cooter||07-03-99||07:47 PM||||Feffer- get an oil filter for the 88 model, try to find an AC Delco or other "name" brand- there is a difference! Any GM product dealer can order parts for any GM vehicle. I work in a body shop and use a local Pontiac dealer to get all my GM parts, but having part numbers will help.They didn't laugh about my fiero parts, they went out of their way to get new brake hoses for the front of my car- No more were in existance! They put in a special order to the OEM supplier and a set were made up just for me! Now I feel special. There was a little note in the hose box that said "I hope these last as long as the originals!" and had the guy's name who made the hose. That was cool.
